Transformer models use attention mechanisms as the core means to process sequences. Attention replaces recurrence and convolution by computing data-dependent weighted sums over the entire input (or parts of it), allowing the model to capture long-range dependencies, parallelize computation across positions, and dynamically route information between tokens. The following explains how attention is formulated, implemented, and used inside a transformer to process sequences.

1. Core idea: queries, keys, values
- Each token in a sequence is represented as a vector (an embedding). For attention, each token produces three vectors: a query q, a key k, and a value v.
- Attention answers the question: for a given query q (corresponding to a target position), how much should it attend to every key k (corresponding to source positions)? The attention output for that query is a weighted sum of the corresponding values v, with weights determined by the similarity between q and each k.

2. Scaled dot‑product attention (standard building block)
- Given matrices Q, K, V (queries, keys, values) with shapes (n, d_k), (m, d_k), (m, d_v) respectively (n = number of queries, m = number of keys/values), the scaled dot-product attention is:
  Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(Q K^T / sqrt(d_k)) V
- The dot products Q K^T produce an (n × m) matrix of pairwise similarities; dividing by sqrt(d_k) stabilizes gradients and softmax behavior for larger d_k; softmax converts similarities into nonnegative weights that sum to 1 across keys; multiplication by V produces the final (n × d_v) attended representations.
- Special-case: when n = m (self-attention), each position attends to all positions (including itself) in the same sequence.

3. Multi-head attention
- Instead of a single attention, transformers use H parallel attention “heads.” Each head projects inputs to head-specific subspaces:
  For head i: Q_i = Q W_Q^i, K_i = K W_K^i, V_i = V W_V^i
  where W_Q^i, W_K^i, W_V^i are learned projection matrices to dimension d_k (typically d_k = d_model / H).
- Each head computes scaled dot-product attention independently, producing outputs of size (n, d_k). Outputs of all heads are concatenated and linearly projected back to model dimension:
  MultiHead(Q,K,V) = Concat(head_1, ..., head_H) W_O
- Rationale: different heads can focus on different types of relationships (syntactic, semantic, positional), enabling the model to capture multiple aspects of token interactions.

4. Positional information
- Attention is permutation-equivariant: without additional signals, it cannot distinguish order. Transformers inject position information into token representations so the model can use order.
- Two common approaches:
  - Sinusoidal positional encodings: deterministic sin/cos functions added to embeddings, encoding relative periodic patterns at different frequencies.
  - Learned positional embeddings: learned vectors added to token embeddings.
- Variants include relative positional encodings (where attention scores are biased by relative distances) that often improve modeling of relative order.

5. Transformer layer structure (encoder/decoder)
- Encoder block: each encoder layer typically contains:
  1) Multi-head self-attention over the input sequence (each position attends to all positions),
  2) Add & Norm (residual connection + layer normalization),
  3) Positionwise feed-forward network (two-layer MLP applied independently to each position),
  4) Add & Norm.
- Decoder block (for sequence generation) typically contains:
  1) Masked multi-head self-attention (causal mask prevents attending to future positions),
  2) Add & Norm,
  3) Multi-head cross-attention over encoder outputs (decoder queries attend to encoder keys/values),
  4) Add & Norm,
  5) Feed-forward,
  6) Add & Norm.
- Stacking multiple layers allows repeated refinement: outputs of one layer become queries/keys/values for the next.

6. Masking and autoregressive decoding
- For autoregressive (left-to-right) generation, the decoder’s self-attention uses a causal mask that sets logits for future keys to −∞ before softmax so a position cannot attend to later positions.
- Padding masks can also be applied to ignore padded positions in variable-length sequences.

7. Interpretability and attention weights
- The softmax weights (attention maps) indicate how much each query attends to each key. These weights are often inspected to understand which tokens the model considers relevant for a prediction. While informative, attention maps are not a perfect explanation of model behavior.

8. Computational characteristics
- Complexity: standard self-attention computes an (n × n) similarity matrix, giving time and memory cost O(n^2 · d) per layer (n = sequence length, d = hidden dimension). This makes very long sequences expensive.
- Advantages: unlike RNNs, attention layers are fully parallelizable across positions, enabling efficient training on modern hardware (GPUs/TPUs).

9. Limitations and scalable variants
- Quadratic cost with sequence length limits context size. Many variants address this:
  - Sparse attention patterns (Longformer, BigBird) limit attention to local windows, global tokens, or sampled tokens.
  - Low-rank approximations / linearized attention (Linformer, Performer) approximate QK^T to reduce cost.
  - Memory/recurrence mechanisms (Transformer‑XL, Compressive Transformers) provide longer effective context by caching compressed past states.
  - Reformer uses locality-sensitive hashing to approximate attention more cheaply.
- Choice of variant depends on trade-offs between full-context modeling and computational constraints.

10. Why attention works well for sequence processing
- Direct pairwise interactions: every token can attend to any other token, enabling direct modeling of long-range dependencies without intermediate steps.
- Dynamic, content-based routing: attention weights depend on token content, not position alone.
- Parallel computation: entire sequences can be processed simultaneously, accelerating training.
- Composability: attention layers combine naturally with feed-forward layers, residual connections, and normalization to form deep, expressive models.

11. Practical implementation notes
- Typical transformer implementations use:
  - d_model (hidden size), H heads, d_k = d_model / H.
  - Linear projection matrices W_Q, W_K, W_V (size d_model × d_k), and W_O (size (H·d_k) × d_model).
  - Layer normalization and residual connections to stabilize training.
  - Dropout applied to attention weights and feed-forward outputs for regularization.
- Training often uses teacher forcing for autoregressive models; inference uses cached keys/values to avoid recomputing attention over the entire history for each generated token.

Summary
Attention in transformers converts sequences into contextualized representations by computing content-based weighted sums of values, using queries and keys to determine relevance. Scaled dot-product attention and multi-head attention provide the mathematical and architectural forms that let transformers model complex, long-range relationships while benefiting from parallel computation. Positional encodings supply order information; masking enables autoregressive generation; and various extensions mitigate quadratic scaling to handle longer contexts. Together, these elements make attention the central mechanism enabling transformers to process sequences effectively across a wide range of tasks.
